# THE CREDENTIALS THIS INSTALLATION IS GIVEN, AND THE ONLY PLACE THEY REACH THE MACHINE.
#
# This file is the TEMPLATE. Generating an installation copies it to secrets/secrets.<stage> beside
# it and fills the keys it was answered for, each one in place, so every value keeps the paragraph
# above it. That copy is the whole of the hand-written input the seed of this installation's Vault
# is read out of.
#
# THE FILLED COPY IS NEVER COMMITTED — NOT TO THIS REPOSITORY AND NOT TO ANY OTHER. Version control
# is told to ignore everything under secrets/ and to admit exactly one path back: this template. The
# copy stays on the machine that was installed, at mode 600, readable by its owner alone.
#
# EXACTLY ONE STEP EVER WRITES THE COPY, and it is the step of the generating run that fills the keys
# that run was answered for. Nothing after it adds anything: the seed READS this file and writes what
# it finds into Vault, and the step that initializes Vault writes a different file altogether. So
# what the copy holds is what an operator typed and what that one step put there, and a value you
# rotate by hand stays rotated.
#
# THIS IS NOT THE FILE THAT CAN UNSEAL YOUR VAULT, AND KEEPING IT AS THE DISASTER COPY LOSES THE ONE
# THAT CAN. Vault's root token and its unseal keys come into being when the store is initialized and
# are written to secrets/vault-<stage>.txt, mode 600 — a SEPARATE file standing beside this one in
# this same directory, which the initializing step tells you in so many words to copy off this host.
# Nothing puts them here, and nothing here can be used to unseal anything.
#
# NOT EVERY CREDENTIAL OF THIS INSTALLATION IS A KEY OF THIS FILE. The identity provider's secret
# key, its two database passwords and the password of its first administrator are generated by the
# seed itself, straight into the secret store, and the mail signing key is left to another writer.
# None of the five is a key here, so none of them is ever typed and none of them can be read back
# out of this file. What carries the four generated ones onto the cluster is a manifest the seed
# writes at mode 600 for the length of one apply and removes again, whether that apply succeeded or
# failed.
#
# THE FIRST ADMINISTRATOR'S PASSWORD IS READ OUT OF THE STORE AND NOWHERE ELSE, at
# secret/<stage>/idp/database under the field `bootstrap-password`. It is what the identity
# provider seeds its `akadmin` account with on its very first start, and it is the answer to
# "how do I log in to the identity provider of a machine that was just installed".
#
# EVERY VALUE IN THIS TEMPLATE IS EMPTY, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. A key counts as unanswered when it
# is empty, when it is absent, AND when it still carries exactly what this template carries. So a
# plausible-looking example on one of these lines would be read as somebody's answer and the
# installation would be built on it. That mistake has already put certificate mail and an identity
# provider's first account on a mailbox nobody reads. An angle-bracketed word is no safer: the
# Vault seed refuses by name any value that still looks like a slot to fill in.
#
# NOTHING CAN GENERATE OR DERIVE ANY OF THESE. Each is issued by a third party — GitHub,
# Cloudflare, Hetzner, Docker Hub — and obtained by hand, once. There is no default and there is no
# fallback: a credential that is not given stops the run, and the refusal names every missing one
# at once rather than one per attempt.
#
# A KEY THAT ALREADY CARRIES A VALUE IS NEVER REWRITTEN. Generating again fills only what is still
# empty — where "empty" also covers a value that is still exactly what this template ships — so a
# credential you rotated by hand survives a second run instead of being reset to the one the run was
# answered with.
#
# A CREDENTIAL HOLDING A DOUBLE QUOTE OR A LINE BREAK CANNOT BE WRITTEN INTO THIS FILE AT ALL. It is
# read back as KEY=value lines twice — by the generator that fills it, and by the Vault seed that
# reads every seeded value out of it. A double quote would end the quoting one line early and turn
# the rest of the file into something a shell reads as syntax, and a line break would split one
# assignment into two. Neither can be escaped into safety for both readers at once, so such a value
# is refused by name rather than mangled. Issue a credential without either character.
#
# LINE ENDINGS ARE PART OF THE VALUE, SO A FILE THAT CAME BACK THROUGH THE WRONG EDITOR IS REFUSED
# WHOLE. The seed stops on the first carriage return it finds anywhere in the file, before it reads a
# single value out of it, and says to rewrite the file with unix line endings. Refused early on
# purpose: a carriage return inside a token produces a rejection from whatever the token is presented
# to, and none of those messages says where it came from.
#
# The first six keys are wanted on EVERY cluster. The four below them are wanted only on the ONE
# cluster that carries the build plane — the cluster whose image registry and builders every other
# cluster of this installation pulls through. The generator offers all ten; only where the answers
# name this cluster as the build plane does it DEMAND the last four and write them, so on every other
# cluster those four keep exactly what this template ships.


# ── the six every cluster is given ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The WRITER on the repository this installation's branch is generated in: a GitHub fine-grained
# access token with Contents read and write on it. It must ALSO carry Contents write on the tenant
# catalog repository named in this installation's configuration, because the cluster manager receives
# this one value under two names and pushes its tenant pointers to the catalog with the second. One
# organisation-wide token covering both repositories is the usual shape.
#
# ONE ENTRY, ONE READER. The seed writes it to secret/<stage>/manager-host/github as `write-pat`,
# which is the cluster manager's own isolated tier: no policy this seed writes lets any other
# workload of the cluster read anything below manager-host, and the manager reaches it through a
# login of its own — a Vault role bound to one dedicated service account in one namespace, and to
# nothing else.
#
# THAT ONE ENTRY BECOMES THREE POD VARIABLES. In the manager itself, GITHUB_WRITE_PAT is what its
# branch comparison and its reset wizard list, compare and delete branches with over the GitHub API,
# and CATALOG_WRITE_PAT is what its tenant registry pushes tenant pointers to the catalog with. In
# the registry reaper job, GITHUB_WRITE_PAT again enumerates install branches and reads consumer
# pointers; that job pushes nothing.
#
# IT IS NOT WHAT A RELEASE PUSHES WITH. The image pins a release bump writes are pushed with the two
# build-plane keys at the foot of this file, which are separate answers standing on separate entries.
#
# Because this token can write a default branch, protect that branch with a repository ruleset — a
# ruleset guards against a mistake by whoever legitimately holds the token, which is a different
# question from who holds it.
GITOPS_REPO_PAT=

# The READER on that same repository: a SECOND GitHub token, Contents read and no write at all. Two
# tokens rather than one is the point: a component that can only read cannot rewrite the books of
# every cluster, and issuing a single token and pasting it into both lines throws that separation
# away without anything reporting it.
#
# WHERE IT LANDS. The seed writes it to secret/<stage>/system/argocd/repo-read-pat as `pat`, on the
# system tier — the tier no policy of this seed grants to any workload at all. What is meant to
# consume it is the reconciler instance of a SLAVE cluster: one such instance runs per slave, in its
# own namespace on the master, and it holds two read-only repository credentials, one for the install
# branch it syncs and one for the tenant catalog it reads its member charts from.
#
# NOTHING ON THIS BRANCH DELIVERS IT THERE, AND THAT IS SAID HERE RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE DISCOVERED.
# Those two credentials are read from secret/<stage>/slaves/<slave>/repo-pat and
# secret/<stage>/slaves/<slave>/deploy-repo-pat — a COPY on the slaves tier, not the entry above —
# and no step of any program of this installation writes either of them. The cluster manager cannot
# write them either: its own policy grants nothing under the slaves tier. So until something creates
# those two entries, a slave's reconciler instance has no repository credential and the secrets it
# needs are never materialized. A first cluster is unaffected, because it has no slave. The key is
# asked for on every cluster all the same: it is the value those entries are to be made from.
GITOPS_REPO_READ_PAT=

# The read credential for the TENANT CATALOG — the repository holding this installation's member
# charts and their per-stage pins, which is a different repository from the one this branch lives in
# and is named by CATALOG_REPO in the configuration beside this file.
#
# TWO THINGS READ IT, AND NEITHER COULD BEFORE THIS KEY EXISTED. deploy-gitops clones the catalog
# onto the machine with it — that checkout is where the programs a machine runs come from — and
# register-slave copies it into a slave's own entry. Both name it as CATALOG_REPO_READ_PAT, and
# deploy-branch and regenerate-branch both fill it here under that name; the key was simply never
# added to this template, so the row that fills it refused and a first installation stopped at it.
#
# READ-ONLY, and for the reason the one above it is: what runs programs on a machine must not be
# able to rewrite the catalog they come from.
CATALOG_REPO_READ_PAT=

# A Cloudflare API token carrying exactly two permissions on your own zone: read the zone, and edit
# that zone's DNS records. Nothing account-wide, and nothing on any other zone.
#
# TWO READERS, one credential. The cluster manager takes it as `api-token` from
# secret/<stage>/app/cloudflare-dns and receives it as the pod variable CLOUDFLARE_DNS_API_TOKEN —
# that is what publishes and withdraws the one DNS record each consumer and each tenant is reached
# at. And the publish-mail-dns program reads it straight out of this file when an operator triggers
# it, to publish a sender domain's SPF, address, DKIM and DMARC records through the same API.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# The PUBLIC half of the mail signing key, as the single line of base64 a DKIM record's p= carries.
# Public on purpose: this exact value is what the record broadcasts to every receiver, so it is no
# secret — it stands in this file because the file is the one hand-filled input an operator fills,
# and the publish-mail-dns program reads it from beside the token above.
#
# LEFT EMPTY, NO DKIM RECORD IS PUBLISHED, and the program says so rather than failing: a DKIM
# record nothing signs with makes receivers fail mail that is otherwise fine. The PRIVATE half is
# the value the seed's mail-relay entry names as owned by another writer; until something generates
# that key pair, this stays empty and the mail DNS stands on SPF alone.
MAIL_RELAY_DKIM_PUBLIC_KEY=

# The hostname of the Hetzner Storage Box this installation stages every dump on — the one remote
# filesystem behind backing a unit up, restoring it, and moving it to another cluster. Hetzner
# writes it in the form uXXXXXX.your-storagebox.de.
#
# THIS VALUE AND THE TWO BELOW IT ARE ONE CREDENTIAL. The cluster manager takes all three as `host`,
# `user` and `password` out of secret/<stage>/app/storage-box and receives them as the pod variables
# STORAGE_BOX_HOST, STORAGE_BOX_USER and STORAGE_BOX_PASSWORD. Answer all three or none: a staging
# area configured halfway is a backup that fails at the moment it is needed and not before.
#
# This one names a machine rather than granting anything, so it is left readable in a dry run where
# the password is hidden — an operator checking a plan has to be able to see which box it would
# write to.
STORAGE_BOX_HOST=

# A SUB-account of that storage box, with SSH/SFTP enabled and a directory of its own; Hetzner
# writes it as uXXXXXX-subN. Never the main account: the main account's password also unlocks the
# Hetzner Robot, and so can delete the machine this installation runs on.
STORAGE_BOX_USER=

# The password of that sub-account, and never the main account's. It is one of the values kept out
# of every plan, log and run record: what a plan carries for it is the KEY the run would fill and a
# redaction marker in place of the value.
STORAGE_BOX_PASSWORD=


# ── the four only the cluster carrying the build plane is given ─────────────────────────────────

# BUILD PLANE ONLY. A Docker Hub username. This installation's own registry runs a pull-through
# cache of Docker Hub: an image it does not already hold is fetched from Docker Hub on demand and
# kept.
#
# ITS READER IS THE REGISTRY. It and the token below are taken as `dockerhub-user` and
# `dockerhub-token` out of secret/<stage>/app/registry and assembled into the credentials file the
# pull-through cache authenticates with, mounted in the registry's own pod. With no credentials in
# that file the cache falls back to an anonymous fetch, which counts against this machine's public
# IP address and is rate limited. Leave this and the token below empty on every cluster that does
# not carry the build plane: nothing there demands them and nothing there writes them.
REGISTRY_DOCKERHUB_USER=

# BUILD PLANE ONLY. A READ-only Docker Hub access token for the account above, issued under Account
# settings, Personal access tokens. The pull-through cache is its only use. Nothing here ever pushes
# to Docker Hub, so a token carrying write permission grants a reach nothing on this machine uses.
REGISTRY_DOCKERHUB_TOKEN=

# BUILD PLANE ONLY. A GitHub token with Contents read and write on the repository this installation
# is generated in — a SECOND answer, and never the same one you gave for GITOPS_REPO_PAT above. That
# separation is the whole point of two answers: this one is reachable from every build namespace on
# the plane, and some of those belong to parties outside this organisation, so rotating it or
# withdrawing it touches nothing the cluster manager does.
#
# The seed writes it, with no stage in the path, to secret/build/hostyour-cloud/repo-pat as `pat` —
# a release is one image and never one per stage, so this credential stands once on the one secret
# store the build plane can authenticate against.
#
# ITS READER IS THE RELEASE PIPELINE'S BUMP TASK, and only that task: the credential set it mounts
# carries one path-qualified git record per repository the bump may push to, and this value is the
# record for the platform repository whose image pins the bump writes. It is demanded rather than
# warned about — writing that pin is the one thing a build plane exists to do, so a missing
# credential must stop the seed instead of surfacing at the first release.
BUILD_HOSTYOUR_CLOUD_REPO_PAT=

# BUILD PLANE ONLY. The same kind of GitHub token, with Contents read and write on the repository the
# platform's own Controller is built from. The Controller is a unit like every unit it onboards, and
# a unit's build reads its clone credential at secret/build/<unit>/repo-pat — so this one is seeded,
# with no stage in the path, to secret/build/hostyour-manager/repo-pat as `pat`.
#
# IT EXISTS BECAUSE OF THE ORDER OF THINGS. The Controller writes that path for every unit it
# onboards, and it cannot write it for the unit whose image it is itself: on a first installation
# nothing has run yet that could. The seed therefore stands in for it, for that one unit and no
# other, and the Controller keeps writing the path for everything it onboards afterwards.
#
# One organisation credential may back this and the two around it. They are three answers so that
# they can be rotated apart, and so that a build namespace belonging to somebody else never holds the
# one that can rewrite this installation's own books.
BUILD_HOSTYOUR_MANAGER_REPO_PAT=

# BUILD PLANE ONLY. The same kind of GitHub token, with Contents read and write on the TENANT
# CATALOG repository this installation was told to use — your own repository holding the tenant
# charts and their per-stage pins. It is seeded, again with no stage in the path, to
# secret/build/catalog/repo-pat as `pat`.
#
# TWO READERS NAME IT. The release pipeline's bump task takes it as the git record for the catalog,
# which is how a release writes the catalog's per-stage pins. The registry reaper job takes it as its
# CATALOG_WRITE_PAT and reads the catalog's chart values over the GitHub API to work out which images
# are still referenced; despite the name it never pushes with it.
#
# NOTE WHICH VARIABLE IS WHICH. The manager pod also carries a CATALOG_WRITE_PAT, and that one is
# GITOPS_REPO_PAT, not this value. Two pods, the same variable name, two different tokens — which is
# why write on the catalog is demanded of BOTH answers.
BUILD_CATALOG_REPO_PAT=

# The two accounts the platform's own registry serves. One reads and one writes: the reading
# credential is put in every namespace that starts an image of ours, the writing one only where a
# release is made. The NAMES are not secret and are not minted — a name that changed on every run
# would lock out whatever holds the last one. Their passwords are minted and never appear here.
#
# WRITTEN BY THE INSTALLER, FROM THE SAME ANSWER THE ACCESS POLICY IS WRITTEN FROM. The registry
# grants by name: installation/profile.yaml carries global.registryPullUser and global.registryPushUser,
# and apps/registry's zot configuration lists exactly those two accounts. A value edited here alone
# leaves the credential naming one account and the policy granting another, and the registry then
# authenticates a caller and refuses it — 403, which is neither present nor absent to a probe.
REGISTRY_PULL_USER=
REGISTRY_PUSH_USER=

# The name the monitoring plane's administrator logs in under. Not a secret and not minted: a name
# that changed on every run would lock out whoever wrote the last one down. Its password IS minted
# and never appears here — an administrator password an operator invents is one they have also
# written down somewhere.
OBSERVABILITY_ADMIN_USER=admin

# The account guarding the monitoring plane's two push endpoints. The middleware in front of them
# checks an htpasswd line, which the chart composes from these two. The NAME is not secret and is not
# minted; the password is minted and never appears here.
OBSERVABILITY_PUSH_USER=push
